      "section": "world",
      "headline": "Israeli election exit polls show tight deadlock between Benjamin Netanyahu and opposition",
      "body": "Israeli television networks reported exit polls showing a tight deadlock between Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu and his opponents. Broadcasters Kan 11 and Channel 13 predicted Netanyahu's political bloc and Gadi Eisenkot's opposition group would win 51 seats each. Channel 12 reported that Netanyahu's allies won 50 seats while the opposition earned 52 seats. Channel 14 projected a stronger performance for Netanyahu with 60 seats. The BBC reported that Eisenkot's opposition bloc won the most seats but lacked an outright majority. A party or coalition needs 61 seats in the 120-seat Knesset to form a government.\n\nNetanyahu spoke to supporters in Jerusalem after 3 a.m. local time. He said official results will show his Likud party as the largest group in Israel. He also said a government that depends on Palestinian parties must not be formed. Meanwhile, opposition leader Yair Lapid said Netanyahu's bloc suffered a total collapse and crushing defeat. Eisenkot is a 66-year-old former military chief who entered politics four years ago. He lost a son and two nephews in the Gaza war.\n\nGiant screens at the Likud event showed videos of Netanyahu alongside United States President Donald Trump. The screens also displayed footage of military strikes. Reports indicate the United States and Israel plan major attacks against Iran after their national elections. The two countries previously conducted joint attacks on Iran in June 2025 and February 2026.\n\nElection officials said overall voter turnout reached 77.7 percent. This was the highest voter participation rate since 1999. Turnout among Palestinian citizens of Israel reached about 80 percent. This figure broke previous records from 1999. Palestinian citizens make up about 21 percent of Israel's 10 million residents. Exit polls showed Arab-led parties Joint List and Ra'am winning a record 18 seats. Ra'am leader Mansour Abbas said both major Israeli political blocs contacted his party before and after the vote. During voting, witnesses saw Likud supporters driving through Jerusalem with party flags. The supporters blared the Islamic call to prayer to warn voters that Arab citizens were casting ballots.\n\nFinal results could take time to confirm. Israel's Central Elections Committee expects 600,000 to 650,000 people to vote at special polling places. These votes account for nearly 10 percent of all eligible voters. Yisrael Beitenu leader Avigdor Lieberman warned that final results could change once military absentee ballots are counted.",
